r"""
CHAIN-005 - An identity-provider account takes over a Rocket.Chat administrator
            past an enrolled second factor, and deactivating that administrator
            does not take the access away

Steps (each proven on its own in stage 11; this script runs them as one attack):
  1. VULN-002  the OAuth meld finds the local account by the address the identity
               provider asserts and never asks whether that address was verified,
               so an IdP account carrying an administrator's address is melded
               into the administrator's account (and its password is reset).
               -> an administrator session, and the real owner locked out.
  2. VULN-043  on the OAuth path the second-factor decision is the account's age,
               not whether it has an enrolled method, so an administrator who did
               enrol TOTP is signed in with no code at all inside the grace
               window (Accounts_TwoFactorAuthentication_RememberFor).
               -> the second factor does not stop step 1.
  3. VULN-030  the login-code path mints tokens outside validateLoginAttempt and
               REST never re-checks `active`, so the documented response to a
               compromise (deactivate the account, which deletes every token)
               does not hold: signing in again through the IdP returns a working
               token for the deactivated administrator.
               -> administrative access that survives the operator's response.

Vulnerable code:
  targets/rocket.chat/apps/meteor/server/configuration/accounts_meld.js:14-31
      (the local account is found by the asserted address)
  targets/rocket.chat/apps/meteor/server/lib/oauth/twoFactorAuth.ts:23-41
      (doesUserRequire2FA decides on the account's age)
  targets/rocket.chat/apps/meteor/server/api/v1/loginCode.ts:45-63 and
      targets/rocket.chat/apps/meteor/server/lib/auth/issueLoginToken.ts:6-11
      (a token issued with no account-state check)

The chain
---------
Each finding is about a different stage of the same login: who the identity is
bound to, whether a second factor is asked for, and whether the account is
allowed to log in at all. Composed, they defeat the whole incident-response
playbook: the password reset is performed by the attack itself, the second factor
is never requested, and deactivation, the one action an operator is told ends a
compromised account's access, is followed by another sign-in through the same
provider that returns a token with the account's administrator rights intact.

What this chain needs that a default install does not have: an OAuth login
provider whose users can set their own email address (a self-managed GitLab, a
custom OIDC provider, any provider with a self-service profile address). The run
enables Rocket.Chat's built-in GitLab backend against a stand-in provider it
starts inside this container, and restores the provider settings afterwards.

Lab safety: the victim administrator is an extra administrator this run creates,
not the workspace's own, because the meld resets the melded account's password
and a re-run must not lock the lab out. The workspace's own admin account is
untouched.

What a successful run proves
---------------------------
An outsider whose only asset is an IdP account that can claim an email address
obtained an administrator session on a 2FA-enrolled administrator with no code,
locked the real owner out, and after the operator deactivated that administrator
the same outsider signed in again and its token still read administrator-only
routes while the account reported active: false and its password login was
refused.

Dependencies: requests, pyotp (pyotp only to enrol the victim's second factor
during setup).
"""
